B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 19 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 22 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 7 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}22 & 19 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}22 & 19 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-82716/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-82716"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-82618/selection/6bfd04e980002fa767d451be729de7f16610caac4d1100330c97f7a2d93a2f78" :answer/id #uuid "ae1271aa-6cb7-5c26-9a09-15e1dd445007" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82618/selection/bee75be51c0391e11ea1bff8554b45e16ab954a3c0690b31cb1a78460459ee2c" :answer/id #uuid "9a69b189-4dd2-57da-9312-bc93fe6e0c92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-82618/selection/c7c