is $C$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $C$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 10 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 18 & -29 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}18 & -29 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 & -29 \\\\ -5 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-82618/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-82618"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-113708/selection/60eadf18022e36714cee17e0479ab6af66a6361ceef7571ec8305b4e57ee6d8f" :answer/id #uuid "09ea9fcf-9a0b-5bf1-844f-7ad327a9b73c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113708/selection/fe2a88bb40c84469c9d4347b9459a49a2043ff3a9a3fd859c6b18594ed560d4c" :answer/id #uuid "c621c76b-b20c-53e3-80db-7819a93cdc47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113708/selection/b70163afa80242b6e07599db9e6649592c88c8df8d6892329736a0a5f2ef7d0f" :answer/id #uuid "d0e02490-3a3e-516d-a4d7-ab737d23bfd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113708/selection/64016bee11c110718ed4fa5affa1338833add0794a386797f22b2a6d8512dc99" :answer/id #uuid "4ad89b6c-af4f-51eb-bdb5-6764171fda27" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113708/selection/655ede254939c8720ff15fa47ebfaa58a37f5de0c0b757a45cc6bf14b9606903" :answer/id #uuid "2d2fee1f-9b07-5d5f-87ae-0449a1b22f6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113708/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "545fbe08-2b44-506d-b061-9b40048958ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-113708/selection/60eadf18022e36714cee17e0479ab6af66a6361ceef7571ec8305b4e57ee6d8f" :answer-field/choices ["q-113708/selection/60eadf18022e36714cee17e0479ab6af66a6361ceef7571ec8305b4e57ee6d8f" "q-113708/selection/fe2a88bb40c84469c9d4347b9459a49a2043ff3a9a3fd859c6b18594ed560d4c" "q-113708/selection/b70163afa80242b6e07599db9e6649592c88c8df8d6892329736a0a5f2ef7d0f" "q-113708/selection/64016bee11c110718ed4fa5affa1338833add0794a386797f22b2a6d8512dc99" "q-113708/selection/655ede254939c8720ff15fa47ebfaa58a37f5de0c0b757a45cc6bf14b9606903"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-113708" :question/id #uuid "858d9af2-f7a3-5372-8435-4e3c1f733534" :question/math-academy-id "q-113708" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-113708/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113708"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-113706/selection/e225c9aa78115453aa969ca449163a7e54c136534537cce5c2f2044a287dca4e" :answer/id #uuid "f0f19fca-cb77-52c1-acca-90697fc20aa0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113706/selection/8ef58749f2db2a4342ba8a4c0597bee84a0f61884bc812d6836040b351a1559e" :answer/id #uuid "64995c4d-9d1a-5ab3-8824-1e7fbeb4ca3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113706/selection/b99d15be8c4865e7c043ce301a48488d6fce148875af2c104c12981465a3f8d0" :answer/id #uuid "0f1b663f-93a2-5d68-8301-e72f7acf83e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113706/selection/09e17d4029686dfa8b652d397aadc8e7068e400d98febfe67adbb2151ce14c26" :answer/id #uuid "a45e63c4-2d20-59c3-b65a-154d78617a6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113706/selection/acd2634c5734b364dc79ce1c6f6b74383fdbd7bdd34ad7b3cb4ab5ffcdda4c96" :answer/id #uuid "a914f6de-3e33-59a1-93ae-37d7f910a9a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113706/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5bd1e220-cd37-5fb8-81e7-98d7c9cd457b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-113706/selection/8ef58749f2db2a4342ba8a4c0597bee84a0f61884bc812d6836040b351a1559e" :answer-field/choices ["q-113706/selection/e225c9aa78115453aa969ca449163a7e54c136534537cce5c2f2044a287dca4e" "q-113706/selection/8ef58749f2db2a4342ba8a4c0597bee84a0f61884bc812d6836040b351a1559e" "q-113706/selection/b99d15be8c4865e7c043ce301a48488d6fce148875af2c104c12981465a3f8d0" "q-113706/selection/09e17d4029686dfa8b652d397aadc8e7068e400d98febfe67adbb2151ce14c26" "q-113706/selection/acd2634c5734b364dc79ce1c6f6b74383fdbd7bdd34ad7b3cb4ab5ffcdda4c96"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-113706" :question/id #uuid "c4e29ed1-163f-5819-ac9d-dcd40872cace" :question/math-academy-id "q-113706" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]},\\,[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B$ are given above. Find $[x]_{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -6 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 7 \\\\ 5 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-113706/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36f8f02f-acbd-5930-bd0c-8675ac868577"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113706"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4838"] :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. What is $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/0dce1fb65e0b82525b6d461b3641b10eb6d3641f590d46af6fa4af9f2e020dc6.png)" :question/worked-solution "Given the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C},$ we find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to $\\mathcal{C}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ 5 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4840"] :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ to $\\mathcal{C} = \\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$ is given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ given that $\\mathbf{x} =2\\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/acdc98dc5a658e7c945dca00c1a1c0a73d87e3dc98db8071a534a4fd7f215c90.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}} .\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)(-4)-6\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $\\mathbf{x} = 2 \\mathbf{c}_1-6\\mathbf{c}_2,$ we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/883431c76485c9fd706fe79c2bf3936946cfb0dba602ebd0df77d6cc9e800665.png)\n\nNow, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{C} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-4841"] :question/problem "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/0dcc93449ac1d14e51850e4a74f639aeb3acd21b720ba2b9deeb2208d9fffeb4.png)" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}} [\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{S}} .\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ As a result, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/50bbd2dd0fa1c0732a01d07c2614ab15549272fcc0b7d686651f0464b3b50f39.png)\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{S}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{S}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{S}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 10\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 10-3\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7276"] :question/problem "The bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the coordinates of the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are given below. Find $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{C}}.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/c6c10ed068cfde656d178c98eb4af979563efc82d589f750b8fa92973f362e2a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\},$ respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/034a2a4079120d719f9061c339899f9aed9b50a765200291ca15c500ef647a70.png)\n\nReducing the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 13 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 11 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066197/assets/8d07812ea170bcbb56264468f4cfd0fa8b88cf4f68f9b50d35f61e9e33de45c3.png)\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,\\mathbf{x}\\,]_{\\mathcal{C}}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}[\\,x\\,]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & 15 \\\\ -5 & -7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
